  "review": "Re-reviewed all 52 allowlisted tests and their production call chains. The 13 Prompt tests and prior Codex/Pi/ledger paths remain database-free. Claude tests directly construct CliDriver, SessionState, Noop emitter and channels; they spawn only temporary Python JSONL fixtures and write temporary transcript files. DSH helper tests use JSON/oneshots; driver tests construct DshDriver, SessionState, Noop and a temporary Python fixture; dsh_tests::transcript_root only creates a temporary directory and sets CODEG_HOME. The two additional shared tests construct in-memory ConnectionManager/SessionState and channels, without AppState or a database handle. No allowlisted path starts a real Agent, provider, SQLite, migration or seed. Actual runner JavaScript is now fingerprinted as embedded executable source: its next-step inject and exact pending-id cleanup occur at reviewed owner hooks outside Session.append; cross-turn filtering precedes user/message append. Separate Node tests exercise fake Agents with the Session publication guard, not the real DSH application. Early real Claude loopback observations are explicitly excluded from this database-free suite. Unlisted database suites remain compile-only. Final re-review: the unavailable/wrong-session Claude init path now drops its stdin handle (Tokio pipe shutdown alone does not close it), and the fake CLI must record real EOF; pure formatting of new DSH/shared files has no call-chain changes. Reviewed 16 additional owned-ACP tests: native_acp uses only JSON, SessionState and Notify/Mutex; its receipt-history test writes temporary transcript files through the existing transcript writer with CODEG_HOME scoped to a temporary directory. Two real SACP reactor tests use in-memory duplex channels and never run the full connection loop. Seven owned-bootstrap tests exercise pure environment planning, private temporary files and worker threads; one OpenClaw bootstrap test exercises pure path/environment planning. They do not instantiate AppState, a database or a real runtime. Reviewed production startup resources include Hermes Python source-hash import hooks and npm loader hooks, and OpenClaw ACP preload/Gateway plugin resources; their separate Python/Node tests use fixed source fragments, fake Agents/processes and real pure ACP SDK routing, never import the full application or SQLite. Fingerprints now cover bundled JavaScript, Python and plugin metadata as well as Rust and Cargo files. ReceiptBatch closes history before successor admission and late acknowledgements remain unconfirmed rather than writing after the successor. Unlisted tests remain compile-only.",
